//? Title: Girvan–Newman: shortest-path shares and batch community cuts
//? Subtitle: Actual Brandes states, tied removals, original-graph modularity and Zachary’s karate network
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A bridge between two triangles carries every shortest path between their two
sides. Removing that bridge reveals two components. Girvan–Newman repeatedly
recalculates edge betweenness to find such boundaries, but the details of removal
and arithmetic matter. This report begins with hand-checkable bridge and equal-path
examples, then follows the native implementation’s simultaneous near-maximum
batches. A four-cycle overshoots a requested two-way split. The original karate-club
graph supplies a real network, with every visited partition evaluated against the
original edges. Actual primitive histories, independent exact-path fixtures and
explicit floating-count and record limits distinguish the method from guarantees
it does not provide.
)

\toc

> Nine cross-pairs must use the bridge

Join triangles {0,1,2} and {3,4,5} by edge 2–3, as in the original example. Each
of the three left vertices can pair with each of the three right vertices. All
nine cross-pair shortest paths use the bridge, so its conventional undirected
betweenness is 9. Edge 0–1 is used only by its own endpoint pair and has score 1.
The four other triangle edges have score 4. Native raw accumulation visits both
source directions, producing twice these conventional values.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gn_bridge.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/girvan_newman.hpp>
#include <datamunge/datasets/network_data.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const auto graph=dm::algorithms::clique_bridge_graph();const auto b=dm::algorithms::edge_betweenness(graph.vertex_count,graph.edges);
 std::print("| edge | actual raw source sum | conventional undirected score |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(std::size_t i=0;i<b.edges.size();++i)std::print("| {}–{} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} |\n",b.edges[i].first,b.edges[i].second,b.raw_scores[i],b.scores[i]);
 const std::array<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint,6> pos{{{-5,-1.5},{-5,1.5},{-1.5,0},{1.5,0},{5,1.5},{5,-1.5}}};
 dm::graphics::Diagram d(65,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.circle=true;node.width=.7;node.height=.7;node.padding=8;node.font_size=22;
 for(std::size_t u=0;u<pos.size();++u){node.fill=u<3?dm::graphics::RGB{219,234,254}:dm::graphics::RGB{254,226,226};d.node(std::to_string(u),pos[u],std::to_string(u),node);}
 for(std::size_t i=0;i<b.edges.size();++i){const auto [u,v]=b.edges[i];dm::graphics::DiagramStroke stroke;stroke.color=(u==2&&v==3)?dm::graphics::RGB{220,38,38}:dm::graphics::RGB{71,85,105};stroke.width=(u==2&&v==3)?4:2;d.link(std::to_string(u),std::to_string(v),std::format("{:.0f}",b.scores[i]),stroke);}
 d.text({0,3.4},"Conventional edge scores: the bridge carries 3 × 3 cross-pairs",24).text({0,-3.1},"Raw native source sums are twice the labels",23).save_svg("build/gn_bridge.svg");
 if(b.raw_scores.at(3)!=18||b.scores.at(3)!=9)throw std::runtime_error("bridge pair count");
}
