\image(figures/gn_diamond.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The diagram labels only source 0’s actual predecessor contributions. Summing all sources and halving gives the separate conventional edge scores; these two quantities are not interchangeable.)
\alttext(A diamond has two routes from source zero to vertex three. The actual source-zero contributions are one-half on the two edges entering vertex three and one-and-a-half on the two edges leaving source zero. Tables retain actual distances, path counts and dependencies.)

BFS stores distances, predecessor lists and counts σs(v). A shortest path to v ends
at one predecessor, so its count is the sum of predecessor counts. Accumulating
in reverse BFS order assigns the edge u–v the contribution

$$
a_s(u,v)=\frac{\sigma_s(u)}{\sigma_s(v)}[1+\delta_s(v)],\qquad
\delta_s(u)\mathrel{+}=a_s(u,v),\qquad R(u,v)\mathrel{+}=a_s(u,v).
$$
\alttext(The predecessor’s fraction of paths to a successor multiplies one plus the successor dependency; that actual contribution increments predecessor dependency and raw edge score.)

The term 1 accounts for v itself; δs(v) accounts for farther targets. Positive
unweighted distances make the predecessor graph acyclic, so reverse order has the
successor dependencies ready. [Brandes’s original dependency derivation](https://doi.org/10.1080/0022250X.2001.9990249)
is the basis of this shared native recurrence. Counts and dependencies here remain
ordered double arithmetic; the later numeric example states their limitations.

> A target is a minimum, and tied batches can overshoot

The original bridge is uniquely maximal. Its one-edge batch yields {0,1,2} and
{3,4,5}, preserving the original example. A four-cycle instead has equal scores
on all four edges. The native batch deletes all four at once: requesting two
communities returns four singleton components. An already disconnected graph can
meet its target without any betweenness computation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gn_batch.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/girvan_newman.hpp>
#include <datamunge/datasets/network_data.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const auto graph=dm::algorithms::clique_bridge_graph();const auto original=dm::algorithms::girvan_newman_run(6,graph.edges,2);
 std::print("Original communities:");for(const auto& group:original.groups){std::print("\n");for(auto u:group)std::print("{} ",u);}std::print("\nOriginal scored batches {}; removed edges {}; component passes {}.\n",original.work.rounds,original.work.removed_edges,original.work.component_passes);
 const std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t,std::size_t>> cycle{{0,1},{1,2},{2,3},{3,0}};const auto t=dm::algorithms::girvan_newman_trace(4,cycle,2);const auto b=dm::algorithms::edge_betweenness(4,cycle);const auto disconnected=dm::algorithms::girvan_newman_run(4,{{0,1},{2,3}},2);
 std::print("\n| case | target | returned components | scored batches | removed edges |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Two triangles and bridge | 2 | {} | {} | {} |\n| Four-cycle, equal maxima | 2 | {} | {} | {} |\n| Already disconnected pairs | 2 | {} | {} | {} |\n",original.groups.size(),original.work.rounds,original.work.removed_edges,t.run.groups.size(),t.run.work.rounds,t.run.work.removed_edges,disconnected.groups.size(),disconnected.work.rounds,disconnected.work.removed_edges);
 std::print("\nCycle raw score per edge {:.1f}; conventional {:.1f}; native keep test uses raw score < maximum - 1e-12.\n",b.raw_scores[0],b.scores[0]);
 dm::graphics::Diagram d(65,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.circle=true;node.width=.7;node.height=.7;node.padding=8;node.font_size=22;const std::array<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint,4> pos{{{0,0},{2,0},{2,2},{0,2}}};
 for(std::size_t u=0;u<4;++u){d.node("before"+std::to_string(u),pos[u],std::to_string(u),node);d.node("after"+std::to_string(u),{pos[u].x+8,pos[u].y},std::to_string(u),node);}for(auto [u,v]:cycle)d.link("before"+std::to_string(u),"before"+std::to_string(v));
 d.text({1,3.6},"Before: one component",23).text({9,3.6},std::format("After: {} components",t.run.groups.size()),23).text({5,1},std::format("Delete all {} ties\nin one batch\ntarget = 2",t.run.work.removed_edges),22).text({5,-1.5},"Simultaneous deletion can skip the requested component count",24).save_svg("build/gn_batch.svg");
 if(original.groups.size()!=2||t.run.groups.size()!=4||disconnected.work.rounds!=0)throw std::runtime_error("batch semantics");
}
