An independent development oracle uses all-pairs distances, enumerates shortest
paths explicitly and splits exact rational pair weights. It agrees with all 78
initial edge scores and all 34 target requests for this pinned graph. Separate
fixtures cover every simple graph through five vertices and every valid target.
Those checks establish implementation agreement on the checked inputs; they do
not turn modularity or shortest-path communities into sociological ground truth.

> Selected operation counts reveal implementation costs

The textbook unweighted dependency traversal can run in O(nm). This preserved
implementation additionally initializes n-slot working arrays for every source
and uses a binary search to find each edge’s score slot. One scored pass therefore
has an O(n²+n m log(m+1)) bound. Input normalization adds O(r log(r+1)) for r raw
records; connected-component passes add O(n+m). At most m scored batches give
O(r log(r+1)+m n²+n m² log(m+1)), plus terminal component work. Scoring scratch is
O(n+m). Normalization can retain capacity for r copied records after duplicates
collapse, so total ordinary auxiliary storage is O(r+n+m), excluding the caller
input and final result. Retained history is additional storage.

The following family has two equal cliques and one bridge. The bridge carries
size² unordered cross-pairs. Each example needs one scored batch to split, so
source slots grow as n². Counted binary-search comparisons expose an extra cost
that is absent from a claim based only on BFS visits. These selected counts exclude
allocation, sorting comparisons, validation and arithmetic FLOPs; they are not timings.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gn_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/girvan_newman.hpp>
